      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During the electroporation phase, hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs) undergo transient membrane perturbation via an applied transmembrane voltage gradient, facilitating targeted genomic integration. The electroporation protocol employs a calibrated pulsed electric field, typically within the 200-300 V/cm range, over a 5-10 millisecond duration, utilizing an electroporation apparatus optimized for nucleic acid delivery. The transfection buffer, replete with Cas9 nuclease and guide RNA (gRNA) specific to the BCL11A enhancer locus, is administered to the HSPCs, which are then exposed to the electric pulse to transiently disrupt the phospholipid bilayer, allowing for the nucleic acid complex to penetrate the cell.\n\nSubsequent to electroporation, the cells are cultured in a cytokine-enriched medium, such as StemPro-34, at a concentration of 1 x 10^5 cells/mL, supplemented with stem cell factor (SCF), Flt3 ligand (FL), and thrombopoietin (TPO). These cytokines engage with their cognate receptors, triggering intracellular signaling cascades that culminate in the activation of transcription factors and the upreg",
